<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lavrentiy Beria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1899">1899</a>–<a href="/years/1953">1953</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Lavrenti Beria</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lavrentiy Beria was a Georgian Bolshevik and Soviet security chief. He led the Communist Party of the Transcaucasian SFSR and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> from the early 1930s, carrying out the purges there, and in 1938 became head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a> under Stalin, running the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a> and mass repressions. He also oversaw the Soviet atomic bomb project. After Stalin's death he was arrested and executed in 1953.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lavrentiy-beria.jpg" alt="Lavrentiy Beria" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown, presumed official · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lavrentiy_Beria_portrait_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lavrentiy Pavlovich Beria was born on 29 March 1899 (17 March in the old style) at Merkheuli, near Sukhumi, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, into a Georgian Orthodox family of the Mingrelian group. His father was a landowner. He attended a technical school in Sukhumi and later claimed to have joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> in March 1917 while a student at the Baku Polytechnicum. In 1920 he enlisted in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheka">Cheka</a> and rose quickly through its ranks. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> invaded the Menshevik-controlled Democratic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and the Georgian SSR was formed, he was deputy chairman of the Georgian OGPU from 1922 to 1924. He then led the repression of a Georgian nationalist uprising in 1924, after which up to 10,000 people were executed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Beria met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> in the summer of 1931, when Stalin rested at Tsqaltubo and Beria took charge of his security. In August 1938 Stalin brought him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> as deputy head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a>, and in November he succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolai-yezhov">Nikolai Yezhov</a> as its head. Under Yezhov the NKVD had carried out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Purge</a>, which imprisoned or executed possibly over a million people. Beria's appointment brought a relative easing: over 100,000 people were released from the camps and the government admitted some excesses, which it blamed entirely on Yezhov. Arrests, torture and executions continued, and Yezhov was executed in 1940.</p><p>After the Soviet invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1939, Beria organised the Katyn massacre of 22,000 Polish officers and intelligentsia. After the occupation of the Baltic states and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> in 1940 he oversaw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> of hundreds of thousands of people to remote areas or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a> camps. He expanded the system of forced labour and set millions of Gulag prisoners to wartime production. In 1943 and 1944 he oversaw the mass deportation of millions of members of ethnic minorities from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, which many scholars describe as ethnic cleansing or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>. From 1945 he oversaw the Soviet atomic bomb project, whose first nuclear test took place in 1949. At his trial it became known that he had committed numerous rapes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Stalin's death in March 1953, Beria became First Deputy Premier and head of the Ministry of Internal Affairs, and formed a triumvirate with Georgy Malenkov and Vyacheslav Molotov. He released the victims of the Doctors' Plot, announced an amnesty that freed 1,200,000 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a>'s 2,750,000 inmates, and returned the Gulag to the Ministry of Justice. His rivals, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikita-khrushchev">Nikita Khrushchev</a>, feared his ambition. On 26 June 1953 he was arrested at a meeting of the Presidium, with the help of Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgy-zhukov">Georgy Zhukov</a> and armed officers. He was tried for treason and other offences and executed on 23 December 1953, and many of his associates were later executed too.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lavrentiy Beria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Lakoba died in 1936, probably poisoned by <b>Beria</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/abkhaz-ssr">Abkhaz SSR</a></li><li><blockquote>Georgia was governed by its Communist Party within the Soviet system, with Stalin and <b>Beria</b> rising from its ranks.</blockquote><a href="/state/georgian-ssr">Georgian SSR</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Lavrentiy Beria</b> led its Communist Party from 1932.</blockquote><a href="/state/transcaucasian-sfsr">Transcaucasian SFSR</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Merkheuli, in what is now <a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.98,41.14&amp;year=1934" aria-label="Merkheuli on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5543 637 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lavrentiy Beria"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5993 907h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5993 907h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.98,41.14&amp;year=1934">What was here in 1934? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
